"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_bulbourethral" = "Bulbourethral gland markers";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_companion" = "Markers entering the spongy urethra directly: %1$@";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_formula" = "Markers entering the prostatic urethra = seminal vesicle markers + prostate markers. Markers entering the spongy urethra directly = bulbourethral gland markers only. The chart rounds to whole markers.";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_prostate" = "Prostate markers";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_scope" = "Picture one line for each gland type, ending where its ducts reach the urethra. Nutrient-containing fluid from the seminal vesicles reaches the prostatic urethra via the ejaculatory ducts. The prostate adds its own fluid there via separate prostatic ducts. Farther along, the bulbourethral ducts deliver lubricating mucus into the start of the spongy urethra. Use 0 to 4 markers per line and count each only where it first enters the urethra. Markers are invented diagram symbols, not secretion volumes, proportions, or timing.";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_title" = "Markers entering the prostatic urethra";
"health_atlas_accessory_gland_entry_vesicles" = "Seminal vesicle markers";
"health_atlas_aii_amacrine_relay_companion" = "OFF branch change: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_aii_amacrine_relay_formula" = "For a diagram input change s, ON change = s and OFF change = −s. Zero means no change from the diagram reference. Matching sizes make the signs easy to compare; these are not measured gains or shares of a divided signal.";
"health_atlas_aii_amacrine_relay_input" = "Rod bipolar input change";
"health_atlas_aii_amacrine_relay_scope" = "In the main dim-light rod pathway, a rod bipolar cell sends input to an AII amacrine cell. Follow two connections out from AII: an electrical gap junction to an ON cone bipolar cell preserves the sign of the change; a glycinergic inhibitory synapse to an OFF cone bipolar cell reverses it in this sketch. The diagram shows only the rod-driven direction of the relay. The numbers are invented diagram units (u), not light levels, voltages, firing rates, or visual predictions.";
"health_atlas_aii_amacrine_relay_title" = "AII relay: ON branch change";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_companion" = "Starting markers left outside the cells: %1$@%%";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_formula" = "If uptake is U percent of the starting pool and return is R percent of the taken-up share, the returned percentage of the starting pool is U × R / 100. The percentage left outside is 100 − U.";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_return" = "Share of taken-up markers returned";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_scope" = "Near an excitatory synapse, an astrocyte process takes up some released glutamate. The astrocyte can convert some to glutamine and return it to the sending neuron for reuse. In this schematic, markers move from the space around the synapse into the astrocyte, then a share returns to the neuron. The returned markers represent recycling, not a new signal. The percentages are invented; they do not represent measured amounts, timing, or biological rates.";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_title" = "Starting markers returned as glutamine";
"health_atlas_astrocyte_recycling_uptake" = "Share of starting markers taken up";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_companion" = "Marker amount near the inlet: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_formula" = "Each round moves one quarter of the marker amount in each zone to the other zone, halving the difference. After n rounds, far-side amount = M × (1 − 0.5ⁿ) / 2, where M is the starting marker amount. Near-inlet amount = M − far-side amount. The graph rounds n to the nearest whole round (halves round up).";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_marker_amount" = "Marker amount arriving from the ileum";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_rounds" = "Mixing rounds";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_scope" = "The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine. It receives contents from the ileum before they continue into the ascending colon. Picture two equal zones in the pouch, one near the ileal opening and one farther away. In this mixing sketch, all the marker starts near the opening, and none enters or leaves during the rounds. The two zones, rounds and marker units (u) are invented. They do not represent anatomical compartments, elapsed time, bowel transit or water absorption.";
"health_atlas_cecal_pouch_mixing_title" = "Marker amount on the far side of the cecal pouch";
"health_atlas_clitoral_route_trace_companion" = "Diagram links remaining: %1$@";
"health_atlas_clitoral_route_trace_formula" = "Count the links traced as a whole number from 0 to 4. Share traced (percent) = 100 × that count / 4. Links remaining = 4 minus that count. The plot rounds the input to the nearest whole link.";
"health_atlas_clitoral_route_trace_links" = "Diagram links traced";
"health_atlas_clitoral_route_trace_scope" = "Sensory fibers from the clitoral glans run in the dorsal clitoral nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve. This schematic follows one side: glans → dorsal clitoral nerve → pudendal nerve → sacral spinal nerve → spinal cord via a dorsal root. The four arrows count equally; they group parts of the pathway and do not mark synapses. The percentage is progress through the schematic, not distance, time, or sensitivity.";
"health_atlas_clitoral_route_trace_title" = "Clitoral sensory route: share traced";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_body_height" = "Height below dome";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_companion" = "Dome area in the drawing: %1$@ u²";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_formula" = "For a drawing width of 4 u, dome rise h, and body height b: dome area = π × (4 / 2) × h / 2. Body area = 4 × b. Dome share = 100 × dome area / (dome area + body area), as a percentage.";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_rise" = "Dome rise";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_scope" = "A club cell is a secretory cell in the lining of small airways. Its rounded apical surface faces the airway lumen. In this side-on drawing, a half ellipse faces the lumen and sits on a rectangle of the same width. The dome rise is measured from the join to the top of the dome. All lengths use invented drawing units (u). The chart compares flat areas in one profile, not a real cell measurement, curved surface area, or secretory output.";
"health_atlas_club_cell_dome_title" = "Dome share of a club cell profile";
"health_atlas_dendritic_cell_outline_companion" = "Tiles in the sketch: %1$@";
"health_atlas_dendritic_cell_outline_formula" = "Outline marks = 12 base marks plus 2 marks per added tile. Tiles in the sketch = 9 base tiles plus the added tiles.";
"health_atlas_dendritic_cell_outline_scope" = "Dendritic cells can have projections and display small fragments on their surface. In this flat sketch, a 3 by 3 block of tiles has room for one extra square tile at the center of each side. Count one mark for each tile edge along the outer outline. Each added tile hides one old edge and exposes three new ones. These invented counts illustrate the outline, not real cell size, surface area, or how much a cell presents.";
"health_atlas_dendritic_cell_outline_tabs" = "Drawn projections (square tiles)";
"health_atlas_dendritic_cell_outline_title" = "Dendritic Cell: Outline Marks";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_basal_offset" = "Distance from basal edge to marker";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_cell_height" = "Basal edge to apical tip";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_companion" = "Apical tip to marker: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_formula" = "For drawn height H and basal offset d, marker position = 100 × d / H as a percentage of height measured from the basal edge. Apical tip to marker = H − d.";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_scope" = "This model follows an open-type enteroendocrine cell in the intestinal lining. Its narrow apical tip contacts the lumen, but secretory granules cluster toward the tissue-facing side, where the cell releases signals. On the drawing, the basal edge is zero and the apical tip is the far end of the same line. A single dot represents the granule region and stays in the basal half. The lengths use arbitrary drawing units (u) and do not measure cell dimensions, granule distributions, or hormone release.";
"health_atlas_enteroendocrine_polarity_title" = "Granule marker position from the basal side";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_base_icons" = "Stem-cell icons at the crypt base";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_companion" = "Empty lining slots in the drawing: %1$@";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_formula" = "Filled slots = the smaller of 12 and (base icons × steps). Empty slots = 12 minus filled slots. The chart rounds steps to whole counts and stops adding icons once the drawing is full.";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_scope" = "Stem cells near the base of small-intestinal crypts renew their population and produce descendants. Many descendants move within the lining toward a villus. In this illustration, stem-cell icons stay at the base and each makes one descendant icon per diagram step to fill one of 12 initially empty slots on the way up. The slots are invented and the steps have no time unit. This is not a model of actual division patterns, cell numbers, or tissue turnover rates.";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_steps" = "Diagram renewal steps";
"health_atlas_intestinal_stem_model_title" = "Lining slots filled by intestinal stem-cell descendant icons";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_companion" = "Spaces reached by both branches: %1$@";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_formula" = "Add the left and right counts. Any total above 10 is overlap. Distinct spaces reached = total minus overlap, so each space is counted only once. The chart rounds the controls to whole spaces.";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_left" = "Spaces reached from the left";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_right" = "Spaces reached from the right";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_scope" = "Langerhans cells in the epidermis have branching extensions (dendrites) that reach between keratinocytes and help sample the local surroundings. To illustrate overlap, picture 10 numbered spaces in a row between neighboring cells. Two branches of the same cell are drawn covering consecutive spaces from opposite ends of the row. One space is one drawing position, not a unit of skin area. The layout and counts are invented and do not measure sampling or predict an immune response.";
"health_atlas_langerhans_dendrite_reach_title" = "Spaces reached by either Langerhans cell branch";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_companion" = "Icons still outside: %1$@";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_formula" = "Icons taken in = the smaller of the starting outside count and the open space count. Icons still outside = starting outside count minus icons taken in.";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_outside" = "Starting outside count (icons)";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_scope" = "Macrophages help clear cellular debris by engulfing it. In this sketch, plain icons start outside an outline, and each drawn space inside can hold one icon. The graph levels off when all the drawn spaces are filled. The spaces and counts are made up; they do not represent real compartments, how much a cell can take in, or how fast it works.";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_spaces" = "Open spaces inside (spaces)";
"health_atlas_macrophage_clearance_tally_title" = "Macrophage: Icons Taken In";
"health_atlas_mast_cell_granule_tally_companion" = "Dots still stored: %1$@";
"health_atlas_mast_cell_granule_tally_formula" = "Released share (%) = 100 times dots outside divided by 20. Dots still stored = 20 minus dots outside.";
"health_atlas_mast_cell_granule_tally_released" = "Released contents (dot icons)";
"health_atlas_mast_cell_granule_tally_scope" = "Mast cells store substances in granules and can release those substances. Picture 20 dots inside an outline, some shown outside after release. The dots stand for contents, not whole granules. The chart shows the percentage of dots outside. The counts and percentages are invented; they do not describe real cells, rates, or what causes release.";
"health_atlas_mast_cell_granule_tally_title" = "Mast Cell: Released Share";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_companion" = "Tiles sampled in both snapshots: %1$@";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_formula" = "With n tiles per snapshot and a shift of d tiles, the overlap is max(0, n − d). Distinct tiles = 2 × n − overlap. Tiles in both snapshots count only once. The chart rounds inputs to whole tiles.";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_offset" = "Second snapshot shift (tiles)";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_scope" = "Microglial tips can extend and retract while the cell body stays in place. Draw a row of 12 tiles for one nearby patch. The first snapshot marks a block starting at the left edge. The second marks an equally wide block shifted to the right. This invented patch compares two snapshots only. It does not model how far or how fast real branches move, what happens between snapshots, or how much material is cleared.";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_title" = "Distinct tiles sampled by microglial tips";
"health_atlas_microglia_surveillance_width" = "Tiles per snapshot";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_companion" = "Separate reserve cell icons: %1$@";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_formula" = "Final nucleus icons inside the fiber = starting nucleus icons + fused descendant icons. Separate reserve cell icons = 8 minus fused descendant icons. The chart rounds the controls to whole icons.";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_fused" = "Descendant icons shown fusing";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_scope" = "Muscle satellite cells sit beneath the basal lamina but outside the muscle fiber membrane. Some descendants can renew that reserve; muscle-forming descendants can fuse with a fiber and contribute their nuclei. The sketch starts with 8 descendant icons. Each one shown fusing adds a nucleus icon inside the fiber; this sketch puts all the others in the separate reserve. That split and the counts are invented, not fixed cell fates, real cell or nucleus counts, or predictions of repair.";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_starting" = "Starting nucleus icons inside the fiber";
"health_atlas_muscle_satellite_fusion_tally_title" = "Nucleus icons inside a muscle fiber";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_addition" = "Length added at the matrix per step (a)";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_formula" = "Advance = n × a, where n counts drawing steps and a is the length added in one step. Mark position = 4 u + advance. The nail bed ends at 10 u on this drawing; beyond that line the mark is on the free edge.";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_mark" = "Plate mark from the matrix: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_scope" = "The nail matrix at the root forms most of the plate. As new plate material joins at the root, a mark on the older plate moves with it toward the fingertip, over the nail bed and then onto the free edge. The drawing places the matrix at 0 u and the mark initially at 4 u. These lengths are invented drawing units (u), and steps are not days. This is not a growth measurement or prediction.";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_steps" = "Drawing steps (n)";
"health_atlas_nail_plate_advance_title" = "Nail plate advance in the drawing";
"health_atlas_open_quantitative_model" = "Numerical model";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_acinar_apical_band_companion" = "Band area in the drawing: %1$@ u²";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_acinar_apical_band_depth" = "Band depth from the lumen edge";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_acinar_apical_band_formula" = "At band depth d, the drawing is w = 2 + (6 − 2) × d / 6 units wide. Band area = d × (2 + w) / 2. Band share = 100 × band area / 24, as a percentage of the whole profile.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_acinar_apical_band_scope" = "A pancreatic acinar cell tapers toward a small shared lumen. Secretory granules gather near that apical end, while the nucleus lies toward the broader base. The side-on drawing is a trapezoid 6 drawing units (u) tall, 2 u wide at the lumen and 6 u wide at the base. A shaded band starts at the lumen edge and extends inward. Its area is a property of the invented drawing, not a real cell measurement, granule count, or estimate of secretion.";
"health_atlas_pancreatic_acinar_apical_band_title" = "Apical band share of an acinar cell profile";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_companion" = "Icons still on the cell side: %1$@";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_formula" = "Luminal icon count = the smaller of the selected arrow count and the starting icon count. Icons left on the cell side = starting icons minus luminal icons. The chart rounds selections to whole counts.";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_scope" = "Paneth cells sit at the base of small-intestinal crypts. Their apical granules release material toward the crypt lumen, helping protect the nearby lining. In this drawing, a line separates the apical cell side from the crypt lumen. Each selected arrow can move one available icon across the line. The icons are bookkeeping symbols: they do not depict intact granules crossing the surface, actual quantities, or how much protection the lining receives.";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_selected" = "Release arrows selected (one icon each)";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_starting" = "Starting icons at the apical side";
"health_atlas_paneth_luminal_release_title" = "Paneth cell release icons in the crypt lumen";
"health_atlas_penile_path_trace_companion" = "Drawn distance left to the urethral opening: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_penile_path_trace_formula" = "Trace position is a percentage: 0 at the bulb and 100 at the opening. Distance traced (u) = 10 × position / 100. Distance left (u) = 10 − distance traced.";
"health_atlas_penile_path_trace_position" = "Position along the drawn path";
"health_atlas_penile_path_trace_scope" = "Trace only the spongy urethra, the urine passage inside the corpus spongiosum. Start at the penile bulb, follow the shaft, and end at the external urethral opening at the tip of the glans. The path is drawn as a single line of 10 drawing units (u), not to scale. The trace measures position on the drawing, not body length, elapsed time, or urine flow.";
"health_atlas_penile_path_trace_title" = "Drawn distance from the penile bulb";
"health_atlas_plasma_cell_secretion_tally_cells" = "Plasma cell count (icons)";
"health_atlas_plasma_cell_secretion_tally_companion" = "Output symbols per cell icon: %1$@";
"health_atlas_plasma_cell_secretion_tally_formula" = "Output symbols = number of plasma cell icons × 3 symbols per icon.";
"health_atlas_plasma_cell_secretion_tally_scope" = "Plasma cells develop from B cells and secrete antibodies. Picture a row of cell icons, each with three identical output marks drawn beside it. Each added icon contributes another group of three marks. The tally illustrates secretion with made-up symbols, not actual antibody counts, concentrations, or secretion rates.";
"health_atlas_plasma_cell_secretion_tally_title" = "Plasma Cell: Output Symbols";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_companion" = "Radius to the inner edge of the sheath: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_formula" = "For soma radius r and gap width w, the inner edge of the sheath sits at radius r + w. Gap area = π × [(r + w)² − r²]. Lengths use arbitrary drawing units (u); areas use square drawing units (u²).";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_radius" = "Drawn neuron body radius";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_scope" = "Flattened satellite glial cells form a sheath around a sensory neuron body in a dorsal root ganglion, next to the narrow extracellular space they help regulate. In this schematic cross-section the inner circle is the neuron body and the outer circle is the inner edge of the glial sheath. Only the space between the two lines is counted; it is neither the sheath itself nor axonal myelin. The gap is exaggerated, and no real dimensions are implied.";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_title" = "Drawn gap area inside the satellite glial sheath";
"health_atlas_satellite_glial_sheath_gap_width" = "Drawn gap width";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_companion" = "Arterial surplus left: %1$@ hu";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_formula" = "Transferred heat = arterial surplus × transfer share / 100, with the share entered as a percentage. Arterial surplus left = starting surplus minus transferred heat.";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_scope" = "Near each testis in the scrotum, the testicular artery and the pampiniform veins of the spermatic cord carry blood in opposite directions. Heat can pass from warmer arterial blood to cooler venous blood without the blood mixing. In a two-arrow sketch, the artery points toward the testis and the veins point away; a cross-arrow shows heat transfer. The model simply moves a chosen share of a fictional arterial surplus into the venous tally. The arbitrary heat units (hu) and share are teaching inputs, not temperatures, measured exchange efficiencies, or fertility predictions.";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_share" = "Share of surplus transferred";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_surplus" = "Example arterial heat surplus";
"health_atlas_scrotal_heat_tally_title" = "Scrotal heat exchange: transferred heat";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_bone" = "Bony wall width: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_diploe" = "Diploë band width (d)";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_fluid" = "Cerebrospinal fluid band width (c)";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_formula" = "From outside in, the bony wall is outer table (1 u) + diploë (d) + inner table (1 u). The full span is bony wall width + fluid band width (c). Membranes are boundary lines with no counted width.";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_scope" = "The cranial vault forms a rigid covering over the brain. In this slice, two compact bone tables enclose spongy bone (diploë). Inside the wall, dura lines the bone, and cerebrospinal fluid between arachnoid and pia helps cushion the brain. Pia follows the brain surface. The model straightens one slice into bands. The arbitrary drawing units (u) are not anatomical measurements and do not predict protection or injury.";
"health_atlas_skull_vault_layers_title" = "Outer bone to brain surface in the drawing";
"health_atlas_splenic_slit_sorting_companion" = "Tokens left on the cord side: %1$@";
"health_atlas_splenic_slit_sorting_formula" = "The 12 tokens need widths of 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 u. Count one pass for every token whose required width is at most the sketch slit width, including an exact fit. Tokens left on the cord side = 12 − passes.";
"health_atlas_splenic_slit_sorting_scope" = "In splenic red pulp, red blood cells can change shape to squeeze from a splenic cord through slits in the wall of a venous sinus. Cells that deform poorly may be held back. Picture a cord on the left, a slit in the middle and a sinus on the right. Each fictional token needs a minimum width to fit through, measured in sketch units (u). The graph counts tokens reaching the sinus side. This width-only rule illustrates sorting on one route; the values are not actual slit dimensions, cell measurements or retention rates.";
"health_atlas_splenic_slit_sorting_title" = "Tokens passing through a splenic slit";
"health_atlas_splenic_slit_sorting_width" = "Sketch slit width";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_companion" = "Icons still inside the C-cell sketch: %1$@";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_formula" = "Icons moved = rows × icons moved per row. Icons still inside = rows × (5 − icons moved per row). The chart rounds the controls to whole numbers.";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_moved" = "Icons moved per row";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_rows" = "Rows of five icons";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_scope" = "Thyroid parafollicular cells (C cells) lie in the outer part of the follicular wall or between follicles. They release calcitonin into the blood, not into the colloid-filled follicle. In this sketch, five icons start in each row inside the C cell. The same number moves from each row toward a nearby vessel on the tissue side. The grid and counts are invented and do not represent real granule counts, secretion rates, or blood levels.";
"health_atlas_thyroid_c_cell_release_grid_title" = "C-cell icons moved toward a vessel";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_companion" = "Outer edge of one wedge: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_formula" = "Inner edge = 2 × π × lumen radius ÷ number of wedges. Outer edge = 2 × π × (lumen radius + 2 u) ÷ number of wedges. The chart rounds the wedge count to a whole number.";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_radius" = "Radius to the colloid-facing edge";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_scope" = "Thyroid follicular cells line a cavity called the lumen. Their inner (apical) face borders the colloid, which stores thyroglobulin. Their outer (basal) face points toward tissue and nearby capillaries. Picture a slice with colloid in the center and equal cell wedges forming a ring 2 u thick. The two edge lengths are arcs in that slice, not surface areas. The circle, wedge counts and lengths are invented; u is an arbitrary drawing unit, not a measurement or a predictor of hormone output.";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_title" = "Colloid-facing edge of one follicular cell wedge";
"health_atlas_thyroid_follicular_faces_wedges" = "Cell wedges in the ring";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_companion" = "Sketch opening height: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_formula" = "With full width w and height percentage p, full height h = w × p / 100. The area is π × (w / 2) × (h / 2) in square sketch units (u²). At zero height the sketch is a line with zero open area. When height equals width, the sketch is a circle.";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_height_percent" = "Height as a percentage of width";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_scope" = "During urination the sphincters relax and the bladder wall contracts. Urine leaves the bladder neck through the urethra and exits through its external opening. Imagine looking across the channel at one slice perpendicular to that route. The open space is drawn as an ellipse with the full width measured side to side and the full height measured top to bottom. The shape and lengths in sketch units (u) are invented; they do not measure real anatomy or predict urine flow, pressure or sphincter behavior.";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_title" = "Urethral lumen area in cross-section";
"health_atlas_urethral_opening_area_width" = "Sketch opening width";
"health_atlas_vaginal_ruga_span_companion" = "Extra span at full flattening: %1$@ u";
"health_atlas_vaginal_ruga_span_formula" = "For a fold height h, each half of the span is the base of a right triangle with height h and hypotenuse 5 u. Span = 2 × square root of (5² minus h²). Extra span at full flattening = 10 u minus the current span.";
"health_atlas_vaginal_ruga_span_height" = "Example fold height";
"health_atlas_vaginal_ruga_span_scope" = "The vaginal lining has transverse folds called rugae. They can flatten as the wall expands. Imagine a profile along the canal that crosses one ridge. The model draws that ridge as a single peak with two straight sides of 5 u each. The span is the straight distance between the two feet of the fold. Lowering the peak increases that span without lengthening either side. All lengths are invented in arbitrary units (u); the model does not measure the canal, tissue elasticity, or how much a real wall can expand.";
"health_atlas_vaginal_ruga_span_title" = "Vaginal wall fold: straight-line span";
"health_atlas_values" = "My metrics";
"health_atlas_vestibular_gland_volume_a" = "Example volume at outlet A";
"health_atlas_vestibular_gland_volume_b" = "Example volume at outlet B";
"health_atlas_vestibular_gland_volume_companion" = "Difference between example volumes: %1$@ vu";
"health_atlas_vestibular_gland_volume_formula" = "Combined volume = volume at outlet A + volume at outlet B. Difference = the larger example volume minus the smaller one.";
"health_atlas_vestibular_gland_volume_scope" = "The paired greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands supply mucus that helps moisten the vestibule beside the vaginal opening. In a two-branch sketch, each gland has its own duct and its own vestibular outlet, labeled A or B. The ducts do not empty into the vaginal canal or urethra. The chart adds fictional mucus volumes for one example interval in arbitrary volume units (vu). It does not estimate all lubrication, actual secretion rates, or gland function in a person.";
